# peer.prf — the unison profile for the four document lanes (phase 13d.11, design in
# desktop_parity_plan.md §5, decisions D1 and D5).
#
# chezmoi TEMPLATE: private_dot_unison/private_peer.prf.tmpl  ->  ~/.unison/peer.prf
# CORRECTED 2026-09-15, and the old wording here said "only". unison 2.54.0 reads a profile from
# ~/.unison/ BY DEFAULT, and the UNISON environment variable overrides that directory. Measured:
# with UNISON pointing at a directory holding probe.prf, `unison probe -testserver` prints
# "Contacting server..."; with UNISON pointing at an empty directory the same command prints the
# usage banner. So the practical point stands unchanged — a `.config` path is never read, which is
# what Critical finding C1 was about — but "only" was wrong, and the round trip in post_13d11.sh
# depends on the override being real, so the two statements have to agree.
# Decision D-C1 chose the default directory over setting UNISON in the shell, and that still
# holds: a UNISON exported for hsync but not for a hand-typed `unison peer -auto` would give the
# two invocations different archive sets, and the second would see no archive and resurrect
# deletions.
# (the old
# comment here named a `.config`-tree path unison never looks in)
#
# The SAME file name on BOTH machines, not desktop.prf here and laptop.prf there. A chezmoi
# source file has one fixed target name, so a per-machine filename would need a mechanism this
# dotfiles source does not use, while the roots below template cleanly off .machine_type. The
# happy consequence is that `unison peer` means the same thing typed on either machine.
#
# Run it INTERACTIVELY:  unison peer -auto
# `-auto` takes the default action for everything that is not a conflict and still asks on every
# conflict, so the first run — about 50 GB laptop to desktop — is one keypress rather than
# thousands. Never -batch and never -force: PLAN.md's invariant list, and the reason is that
# these four directories are the only copy of some of what is in them.
#
# ONE WARNING BEFORE ANYTHING BELOW, and it changes how every other line here should be read:
# unison SILENTLY IGNORES an option name it does not recognise. A misspelt name on the left of an
# `=` is not an error, it is a setting that quietly does not apply, and unison never mentions it.
# The measurement is written out in full in the backups section at the bottom of this file.
#
# What a single mistyped name would cost, line by line. This is reasoning from what each option
# does, not a second measurement, so read it as ASSUMED except where it says otherwise:
#   ignore     the D5 exclusion stops applying, and 515 MB of irreplaceable beamline data gets
#              copied onto the laptop on the next run, without a word.
#   backup, backuploc, backupdir, maxbackups
#              no recoverable copy of anything unison replaces or deletes. The whole of H3 is
#              undone and the sync looks entirely normal while it happens.
#   path       ONE mistyped `path` line quietly drops that one lane from the sync while the other
#              three still limit the run. That is the realistic case and the nastier one, because
#              nothing about the run looks wrong. Lose ALL FOUR — a rename, a careless
#              search-and-replace — and nothing limits the run at all: the root is the whole of
#              /home/filipejunqueira, so the profile is pointed at the entire home directory. Same
#              shape as the `unison -path` trap in CLAUDE.md's corrections log: a line that looks
#              like it narrows the run.
#   times      modification times stop being propagated. Cosmetic, and visible.
#   confirmbigdel
#              nothing changes, because true is already the default.
#   root       probably the one that would not stay quiet, since a profile with no roots has
#              nothing to sync at all — but that was not tested either.
#
# And there is nothing anyone can run beforehand that would catch any of it. `-testserver` returns 0
# on a profile with a bogus option name (VERIFIED). `post_13d11.sh` only checks that this file
# exists, and says so itself at its line 530 — the round trip never names a profile, so it does not
# exercise this file at all. What was measured on 2026-09-15 is narrower than "nothing reads it"
# and is enough: of every shell script under `pending/` and `tests/`, not one reads an option name
# out of this file. Some of them copy it about; none looks inside it. So the ONLY proof that a line
# here does what it says is watching unison behave — the two ignore lines were proved that way,
# against two throwaway local replicas, and everything else waits for the real run at PLAN.md
# step 18b.

# ── roots ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The local root is written out rather than left as ~ so that it is the same string whatever shell
# or service invokes unison. Both machines are uid 1000 with /home/filipejunqueira.
root = /home/filipejunqueira
root = ssh://{{ if eq .machine_type "laptop" }}desktop{{ else }}laptop{{ end }}//home/filipejunqueira
# The double slash after the host is unison's syntax for an absolute remote path, not a typo.
# `desktop` and `laptop` are Host entries in the shared dotfiles-private ssh config (D7).

# ── the four lanes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Anything not named here is outside the profile entirely: ~/code is git's, the dotfiles are
# chezmoi's, and the big data (~/blender_files, ~/audio, ~/music, ~/videos) is desktop-only or
# laptop-only on purpose and has no lane at all.
path = documents
# career is ONE-SIDED today and the warning used to sit 72 lines below this, inside a paragraph
# about backups (step 31, G9 site 9, 2026-09-20). The desktop has no ~/career and no ~/inbox at
# all, so the first `unison peer -auto` copies them laptop to desktop in full — part of the roughly
# 50 GB named at the top of this file. That is intended, not a conflict; it is said here because
# this is the line a reader meets.
path = career
path = research
path = inbox

# ── what never travels ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ignore = Name .git
# The two patterns below were watched working on 2026-09-15 rather than read off the manual: two
# local replicas, unison's own state pinned to a scratch directory, files planted on one side only
# (log pending/13d.11/logs/desktop_state_20260915-221542.log, section `== U-3`). A `.tmp` file and
# a `~` file were both left alone, so the brace alternation `{*.tmp,*~}` IS understood by 2.54.0
# inside a Name pattern — do not "tidy" it into two separate ignore lines. Of everything planted,
# only the one plain `.txt` file was proposed, and nothing was propagated. The `.git` line above
# was not part of that test.
ignore = Name {*.tmp,*~}

# D5. ~/research/i09-jan-2023-data is 515 MB of beamline data that row 10.5 put on the DESKTOP,
# and it is irreplaceable. It has no business being copied onto the laptop, and a lane that
# quietly does it once is a lane that can quietly undo it later. Ignoring the path by name is the
# cheapest way to say so. If it ever should sync, that is a decision with a date on it, not an
# edit in passing.
# The same 2026-09-15 test planted a file inside that directory AFTER the profile was written, and
# unison still ignored it — so the Path form covers what appears later, not only what was there
# when the line was added.
ignore = Path research/i09-jan-2023-data

# ── behaviour ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Modification times are propagated, so the two sides stop looking different to every tool that
# reads an mtime — rsync previews, backup selectors, `ls -t`.
times = true

# ── backups (H3) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No `backup` line meant no recoverable copy of anything unison replaced or deleted, over lanes
# the file itself calls the only copy of some of what is in them. `backup = Name *` backs up every
# file; `backuploc = central` (the spelling `unison -help` actually names) sends them to
# `backupdir` instead of sitting next to the original. Every option name in this profile — root,
# path, ignore, times, backup, backuploc, backupdir, maxbackups, confirmbigdel — was checked
# against the installed 2.54.0 binary's own `-help` on 2026-09-15, and every one of them is there
# (log pending/13d.11/logs/desktop_state_20260915-221542.log, section `== U-1`). `backuplocation`,
# the spelling the man page's PROSE uses, is not.
#
# AND UNISON SAYS NOTHING WHEN A NAME IS WRONG. Measured the same day (same log, section `== U-2`):
# a copy of this profile with `backuploc` changed to `backuplocation` ran all the way to "Nothing
# to do" — no error, no warning, no mention of the unknown key. Unison ignored the line and used
# its default instead. `-testserver` on that same broken profile returned 0, so `-testserver`
# proves the CONNECTION and never the profile. No gate this project owns would catch a typo in an
# option name here. The only proof that a line in this file does what it says is watching a real
# sync behave, at PLAN.md step 18b.
#
# `backupdir` is `~/.unison-backups`, a sibling of the four synced lanes directly under root, not
# inside documents, career, research or inbox — none of the `path =` lines above name it, so unison
# never walks into it and the backups cannot be synced into themselves. `maxbackups` keeps the last
# 3 generations of a file (default is 2).
#
# ONE ABSOLUTE PATH, BUT TWO DIRECTORIES. `backupdir` is a literal string and the same string is
# rendered into both machines' copies of this profile. It does not mean "whichever home this side
# has"; it happens to be right on both only because both machines are uid 1000 with a home at
# /home/filipejunqueira. A backup is written on the side where the file was overwritten, so after a
# while there are two of these directories holding different things — the laptop's holds what the
# laptop lost, the desktop's holds what the desktop lost. Neither is a copy of the other, and
# looking in one does not tell you what happened on the other. ASSUMED, not measured: `-help` says
# only that `backuploc = central` decides "where backups are stored" and says nothing about which
# replica stores them. The way to settle it is to look on both machines after the first run that
# actually replaces a file.
#
# THAT DIRECTORY DOES NOT EXIST YET, and no script in this batch creates it. Both machines were
# checked on 2026-09-15 and both said ABSENT (same log, sections `== c1` for the desktop and
# `== L-b` for the laptop). Unison itself is what makes it, and it makes one on EACH machine,
# since this same profile is rendered to both. ASSUMED, not measured: that unison creates the
# directory when it needs it rather than failing for want of somewhere to put a backup.
#
# WHEN it first appears is probably NOT the first sync. `backup = Name *` backs up what unison
# REPLACES or DELETES, and the first run is overwhelmingly new files arriving on the desktop: it
# has no ~/inbox and no ~/career at all, and about 50 GB moves laptop to desktop. A new file
# replaces nothing, so it produces no backup. Expect this directory to be empty or nearly empty
# after the big first run, and to fill over the ordinary runs afterwards, as the same files get
# edited on one side and overwritten on the other.
#
# Those later runs are where the unbounded growth is, and it is the one write in 13d.11 with no
# ceiling. `maxbackups = 3` caps each individual FILE at three old generations; it caps nothing
# about the directory. Across four lanes holding roughly 50 GB, the more files that get edited over
# time, the more files have three old copies sitting in here, and nothing in this profile or
# anywhere in 13d.11 ever prunes them. `post_13d11.sh`'s header carries the same fact, so a reader
# finds it from either direction.
backup = Name *
backuploc = central
backupdir = /home/filipejunqueira/.unison-backups
maxbackups = 3

# confirmbigdel defaults to true, but the review found the POST hitting it as a surprise; this
# line just says so. What it does depends on HOW unison is run, which the old wording here flattened
# into "aborts" — `unison -doc confirmbigdel`, read 2026-09-15: "Unison will request an extra
# confirmation if it appears that the entire replica has been deleted, before propagating the
# change. If the batch flag is also set, synchronization will be aborted." So under the documented
# `unison peer -auto` it ASKS, and only under `-batch` does it abort. That is exactly why
# post_13d11.sh's round trip passes `-confirmbigdel=false`: it IS a `-batch` call, over two
# throwaway roots, and an abort there would stop the deletion leg the check exists to prove. The
# doc also notes the flag affects the text interface only. It stops a sync that would empty an
# entire path root on one replica, rather
# than treating a whole-lane wipe as an ordinary delete.
confirmbigdel = true
